# The oldest Aether release that can build fight_flash_fraud.
#
# This is a FLOOR, not a preference. Anything newer on PATH is accepted
# as-is: a contributor sitting on 0.540 is NOT asked to re-fetch just
# because a nicer release exists. Bump it only when this repo starts
# calling a primitive an older Aether lacks, in the SAME commit that
# introduces the call — never speculatively.
#
# Why 0.538.0: `std.spec` — the BDD framework the tests in tests/unit and
# tests/ui are written against — first shipped in the stdlib at 0.538.0,
# absorbed from the now-retired standalone aeocha repo. Before that
# release the specs needed an aeocha CHECKOUT on AETHER_LIB_DIR; they no
# longer do, and this repo no longer clones one. That is the whole reason
# the floor sits where it does.
#
# Two adjacent absorptions, for whoever bumps this next — neither is used
# here yet, so neither raises the floor today:
#   0.538.0  std.os.testing + std.http.client.httptest (process- and
#            HTTP-shaped matchers)
#   0.540.0  std.mutation (Tier-1 mutation testing, from aeocha's
#            contrib/mutate — the last technology blocking aeocha's
#            archival)
# Adopting std.mutation in this repo's test story would move this floor
# to 0.540.0.
#
# See AETHER_FETCH for WHICH release to install when this floor is not
# satisfied — deliberately a different number on a different clock.
#
# Raised 0.538.0 -> 0.766.0 (2026-10-03). A POLICY floor: 0.766.0 is the floor
# Paul set for every Aether-family repo, so they all build against one known
# release. Nothing here needs a primitive newer than 0.538.0. Verified on the
# installed 0.766.0 release (macOS arm64): make all clean, make test green
# (unit + AetherUIDriver UI), and a real `f3 write` / `f3 read` of a 1 GB test
# file: 0 bytes lost.
#
# Raised 0.766.0 -> 0.778.0 (2026-10-05). A TRACKING bump, not a new-primitive
# floor: it follows the family-wide floor up to 0.778.0. Verified on the
# released 0.778.0 (macOS arm64) with the full suite: make all, make test
# (unit 4/4 + AetherUIDriver UI 2/2), and a real `f3 write` / `f3 read` of two
# 256 MB files (0 bytes lost; a flipped byte is caught as 1 bad sector).
# The same change ports write_h2w_file's fs.pwrite call to the byte[]-slice
# form (aether #2301, 0.758): the old 4-arg (file, data, len, offset) shape
# no longer type-checks on 0.758+, so `make all` actually failed on 0.766.0
# too despite the note above. The code's own strict need is therefore now
# 0.758.0 (std.spec 0.538.0 + the slice pwrite), still below this floor.
0.778.0
